<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Contextual Markov Decision Processes (CMDPs) provide a principled framework for sequential decision-making under external signals, but existing methods struggle to generalize with high-dimensional or unstructured context, causing excessive computation and unstable performance. We propose an information-theoretic summarization approach that leverages large language models (LLMs) to compress contextual inputs into low-dimensional, semantically meaningful summaries. These summaries augment states, preserving decision-critical cues while filtering redundancy.
Grounded in approximate context sufficiency, we present— to our knowledge—the first regret bounds and latency–entropy characterization for CMDPs, clarifying the trade-off between informativeness and computational cost.
Across diverse discrete, continuous, visual, and recommendation benchmarks, our method consistently surpasses raw-context and non-context baselines, improving reward, success rate, and sample efficiency while reducing latency and memory usage.
These results position LLM-driven summarization as a scalable and interpretable strategy for efficient decision-making in context-rich, resource-constrained environments.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sequential decision-making lies at the heart of artificial intelligence, with Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) serving as its canonical formalism. By modeling state transitions induced by actions and the resulting rewards, MDPs have powered advances in reinforcement learning across domains such as autonomous driving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kiran et al. (2022)</a></cite>, robotics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kober et al. (2013)</a></cite>, healthcare <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and strategic games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silver et al. (2017)</a></cite>. Yet, this abstraction rests on a simplifying assumption: that the environment’s dynamics depend solely on the current state-action pair. In practice, however, real-world decision-making is rarely so self-contained. Exogenous, time-varying signals play a critical role in real-world decision-making. Examples include weather patterns that affect navigation, patient histories that shape medical outcomes, and evolving user preferences in recommender systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2010)</a></cite>. These signals influence both transitions and rewards, yet they fall outside the immediate state description. Capturing and exploiting such contextual information is therefore essential for aligning learning systems with the complexities of real-world environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, Contextual MDPs (CMDPs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hallak et al. (2015)</a></cite> extend the classical formulation by incorporating contextual variables that modulate dynamics and rewards. CMDPs have been widely applied in domains where environment dynamics are influenced by latent or external factors, including personalized recommendation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2010)</a></cite>, adaptive healthcare treatment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gottesman et al. (2019)</a></cite>, and multi-agent coordination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lowe et al. (2017)</a></cite>. Early formulations typically assumed access to low-dimensional structured context variables. More recent work has expanded CMDPs to settings involving high-dimensional observations, such as natural language, visual streams, or user behavior logs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tennenholtz et al. (2023)</a></cite>, thereby broadening their relevance but also magnifying the associated challenges. While CMDPs enrich the expressive capacity of MDPs, they also expose a central tension: agents must balance <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">expressivity versus tractability</span>. On one side, ignoring relevant context risks shortsighted or suboptimal behavior. On the other, directly ingesting raw, high-dimensional context burdens the agent with noise and redundancy, inflating sample complexity, inference costs, and overfitting risks. Effectively managing this trade-off is critical for deploying CMDP-based agents in complex environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prior attempts to address this challenge can be grouped into three complementary directions. First, theoretical modeling has provided the mathematical foundations of CMDPs and their information-theoretic extensions. Researchers have analyzed sample complexity, derived regret bounds, and identified conditions under which context is sufficient for optimal decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hallak et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>. These results clarify when context helps and offer guarantees on how it should be used. Second, representation learning and memory-augmented methods have sought practical ways to incorporate complex signals. Neural encoders transform raw inputs such as text or images into compact latent features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tennenholtz et al. (2023)</a></cite>, making them more manageable for reinforcement learning agents. In parallel, memory-based architectures and benchmarks like CARL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benjamins et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmied et al. (2024)</a></cite> enable agents to retrieve and reuse relevant episodes from past experience. Such approaches improve scalability, but often struggle with interpretability, potential information loss, and instability under distributional shifts. Third, the integration of large language models (LLMs) represents the most recent direction. LLMs excel at abstraction, reasoning, and natural language understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which has inspired their use as information processors, reward designers, and policy guides <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pternea et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsai et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a></cite>. For instance, LLMs have been used to summarize task instructions, shape reward functions, or propose candidate actions. These applications highlight their ability to bridge symbolic reasoning with trial-and-error learning. However, most existing work emphasizes instruction following or trajectory planning, rather than offering a principled framework for context modeling in CMDPs. This motivates our central question: Can LLM-based summarization, grounded in information theory, provide a scalable and theoretically sound foundation for context-aware decision-making?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Summarization-based Context-Aware MDP</span>, a framework that bridges advances in large language models (LLMs) with the theory of CMDPs. At a high level, our method reframes CMDP agents as <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context-sufficient agents</span>: decision-makers that rely not on raw context, but on compact and semantically meaningful summaries distilled from it. These summaries augment the agent’s state, preserving decision-relevant information while suppressing redundancy. To provide theoretical grounding, we develop an information-theoretic perspective on context sufficiency. Specifically, we analyze the trade-off between the informational value of context and the computational cost of representing it. Our findings highlight a central principle: overly rich context can hinder efficiency by introducing noise and redundancy, whereas appropriately summarized context enables agents to achieve both efficiency and expressivity. This perspective offers principled guidance for designing agents that learn to decide with just enough information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methodological novelty:</span> We introduce a novel CMDP framework that leverages LLM-driven summarization to generate compact, task-relevant context representations. This bridges advances in large-scale representation learning with sequential decision-making.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical grounding:</span> We formalize the concept of <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context sufficiency</span> using entropy and mutual information, and establish regret bounds that quantify the minimal amount of context required for efficient learning. Our framework thus connects context modeling with information-theoretic principles.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical validation:</span> We conduct extensive evaluations across diverse CMDP benchmarks, including navigation, continuous control, and personalized recommendation, showing that summarization-based agents consistently outperform both non-contextual and raw-context baselines in terms of decision quality and computational scalability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretability and generality:</span> Beyond performance, our method produces semantically meaningful summaries that enhance interpretability, and can be readily extended to multimodal settings, offering a general-purpose approach to context-aware decision-making.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By unifying large-scale representation models with information-theoretic decision analysis, this work positions context summarization as a promising paradigm for next-generation CMDP agents. More broadly, our results suggest that effective decision-making in high-dimensional, context-rich environments hinges not on consuming all available information, but on <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">learning to decide with just enough</span>. This principle provides new methodological support for scalable, interpretable, and theoretically grounded reinforcement learning in real-world applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Theoretical Modeling</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contextual Markov Decision Processes.</span>
The contextual Markov decision process (CMDP) framework was first formalized by Hallak et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hallak et al. (2015)</a></cite>, extending the classical MDP setting by introducing context as side information that parametrizes both transition kernels and reward functions. This extension allows policies to adapt to varying environmental conditions and external factors. Subsequent theoretical advances have focused on characterizing the complexity of CMDP learning. Deng et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2024)</a></cite> demonstrated that when agents exploit context-dependent feature representations, algorithms can achieve polynomial sample complexity with provable suboptimality guarantees, thereby providing strong theoretical evidence for the importance of context in efficient learning. Beyond static contexts, Tennenholtz et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tennenholtz et al. (2023)</a></cite> introduced the notion of <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dynamic CMDPs</span>, where the context evolves as a function of the agent’s history, and proposed history aggregation techniques to enable tractable learning with regret guarantees. These works collectively highlight the importance of context in real-world sequential decision-making. However, scalability remains a major challenge: when contexts are high-dimensional, continuous, or multimodal, CMDP algorithms often become computationally intractable, limiting their applicability in complex domains such as robotics, healthcare, and recommender systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Information-theoretic perspectives.</span>
Parallel to these modeling and architectural advances, information-theoretic approaches have been used to formalize the notion of <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context sufficiency</span>. Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite> proposed maximizing mutual information between latent context and task representations to ensure that context embeddings are sufficient for policy conditioning. This line of work connects CMDPs to a broader set of principles such as the information bottleneck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tishby et al. (2000)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemi et al. (2017)</a></cite>, rate-distortion theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berger (1971)</a></cite>, and regret analysis in reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaksch et al. (2010)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al. (2019)</a></cite>. In particular, mutual information has been employed to quantify the informational value of context in reducing state uncertainty <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achille &amp; Soatto (2018)</a></cite>, while entropy is often used to capture the cost of representing contextual signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shannon (1948)</a></cite>. Extending this perspective, Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> demonstrated that saliency-derived indicators in audio large language models (LLMs) can be leveraged as unsupervised quality signals, enabling reinforcement learning optimization without labeled data. This shows how information-theoretic sufficiency measures can be operationalized in practice, bridging abstract CMDP theory with scalable adaptation in multimodal environments. Regret analyses further highlight the inherent trade-off between exploiting richer context for improved decision quality and the increased computational burden of processing it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Russo &amp; Roy (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kakade et al. (2020)</a></cite>. However, existing information-theoretic methods often remain abstract and underexplored in practice, rarely leveraging the representational power of modern LLMs to achieve both theoretical rigor and practical scalability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ortega et al. (2021)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Representation learning and Memory augmented approaches</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Representation learning for context.</span>
To overcome scalability bottlenecks, one important research direction has been to use representation learning to map raw contextual signals into compact embeddings.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modi et al. (2018)</a></cite> proposed linear approximation models and latent encoders to compress side information into low-dimensional representations. While such methods reduce computational complexity, they often rely on restrictive smoothness or linearity assumptions, and may fail to generalize in complex, heterogeneous environments. More recent approaches employ neural encoders to automatically extract latent features, improving scalability but raising concerns about interpretability and information loss when critical semantic cues are discarded <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al. (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Welling (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajkomar et al. (2018)</a></cite>. Despite these advances, purely representation-based methods lack explicit mechanisms to quantify the sufficiency of the extracted context, leaving open the question of how much contextual information is necessary for optimal decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mikolov et al. (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. (2019)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory-augmented architectures and benchmarks.</span>
Another prominent line of research employs memory augmentation to address contextual complexity by selectively retrieving relevant historical information. Schmied et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmied et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposed retrieval-augmented decision transformers, which use external memory modules to dynamically access past sub-trajectories, enabling efficient in-context learning without requiring prohibitively long input sequences. Complementary to this, the CARL benchmark suite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benjamins et al. (2021)</a></cite> has provided systematic evaluation protocols for contextual representation learning across diverse environments, demonstrating the importance of explicit context modeling for zero-shot generalization and robustness. However, as noted by Zisman et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zisman et al. (2024)</a></cite>, memory-based methods face challenges in continuous control settings, where retrieval strategies must balance relevance, diversity, and computational cost. Moreover, the interpretability and stability of retrieved context under distribution shifts remain open problems. These limitations suggest that neither representation learning nor memory-based architectures alone provide a complete solution for scalable, robust context handling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>LLMs in reinforcement learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of large language models (LLMs) with reinforcement learning has emerged as an exciting new paradigm. Pternea et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pternea et al. (2024)</a></cite> categorize this research into three broad interaction modes: RL for LLMs, LLMs for RL, and hybrid frameworks. Within the LLMs-for-RL paradigm, LLMs have been applied as information processors, reward designers, and policy guides <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al. (2025)</a></cite>. For instance, LLMs have been used to generate subgoals, design reward functions, and provide policy-level reasoning signals, effectively bridging symbolic reasoning with trial-and-error learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luketina et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsai et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2023)</a></cite>. The compositional and hierarchical nature of natural language enables LLMs to encode prior knowledge, domain instructions, and structured abstractions that are difficult to represent in traditional RL architectures. Despite these advantages, most existing works have focused on instruction following or trajectory planning, rather than principled integration of LLMs into formal CMDP frameworks. This leaves open the question of whether LLM-based summarization can systematically address the scalability and sufficiency challenges of context in CMDPs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, prior research spans theoretical analyses of CMDPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hallak et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tennenholtz et al. (2023)</a></cite>, representation learning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modi et al. (2018)</a></cite>, memory-augmented architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmied et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benjamins et al. (2021)</a></cite>, LLM-based reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pternea et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and information-theoretic formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Despite these advances, existing methods either struggle with scalability, sacrifice interpretability, or lack principled guarantees of sufficiency. A unified framework that can efficiently handle high-dimensional contexts while providing theoretical grounding is still missing, motivating the present work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Integrating Summarized Context into MDPs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A standard MDP is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\langle\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,R,\gamma\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\langle\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,R,\gamma\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> with state
space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, action space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, transition kernel <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, reward <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, and
discount <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We augment the base state with a learned summary <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and form
the augmented state <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}_{t}=(S_{t},C_{t})\in\tilde{\mathcal{S}}=\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}_{t}=(S_{t},C_{t})\in\tilde{\mathcal{S}}=\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Integrating Summarized Context into MDPs ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows an overview of the summarization-based Contextual MDP workflow.
At each step, the agent observes the state <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, historical interactions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and exogenous
signals <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are summarized by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> into a compact context <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The augmented state
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{t},C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{t},C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is then used for action selection and value estimation. This overview precedes the
formal definitions and objective in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Problem Reformulation and Design Requirements ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01620v1/detailed_cmdp_architecture.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/309;" width="548" height="309"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the summarization-based Contextual MDP (CMDP) framework.
Summaries <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> condense history <math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and exogenous signals <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to guide
action selection and transitions. The dashed loop indicates continual summary updates.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(S;C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(S;C)</annotation></semantics></math> for the stationary mutual information between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(S_{t};C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(S_{t};C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for the per-step variant. All context variables are written as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Problem Reformulation and Design Requirements</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">High-dimensional or unstructured context can inflate sample complexity and latency. We cast learning <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as an <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">approximate sufficiency</em> problem that maximizes informational value while controlling complexity:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\psi,\pi}\;I_{\phi}(S;C)\;-\;\lambda\,H(C_{t})\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})\leq\mathcal{B},\;\mathrm{Tokens}(C_{t})\leq\mathcal{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>Latency</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Tokens</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\psi,\pi}\;I_{\phi}(S;C)\;-\;\lambda\,H(C_{t})\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})\leq\mathcal{B},\;\mathrm{Tokens}(C_{t})\leq\mathcal{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Desiderata: (i) Bellman consistency under Markovian augmentation; (ii) explicit token/latency budgets; (iii) robustness to update frequency and perturbations; (iv) empirical links among <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(S;C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(S;C)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, regret, and latency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Summarization-Based State Augmentation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t}=\{(S_{\tau},A_{\tau},R_{\tau})\}_{\tau&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t}=\{(S_{\tau},A_{\tau},R_{\tau})\}_{\tau&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be exogenous signals. A summarizer <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{t},E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{t},E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> into</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}=g_{\psi}(H_{t},E_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}=g_{\psi}(H_{t},E_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We assume the following <em id="S3.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pre-action</em> timing: <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed before sampling <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is refreshed after observing <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{t+1},R_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{t+1},R_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The value relations are</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{k\geq 0}\gamma^{k}R(\tilde{S}_{t+k},A_{t+k})\mid\tilde{S}_{t},\pi\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{k\geq 0}\gamma^{k}R(\tilde{S}_{t+k},A_{t+k})\mid\tilde{S}_{t},\pi\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=R(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})+\gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{S}_{t+1}\sim\tilde{P}(\cdot\mid\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})}[V^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t+1})]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})+\gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{S}_{t+1}\sim\tilde{P}(\cdot\mid\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})}[V^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t+1})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with augmented dynamics and reward</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{P}(\tilde{S}_{t+1}\mid\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{P}(\tilde{S}_{t+1}\mid\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(S_{t+1}\mid S_{t},A_{t},C_{t})\cdot P(C_{t+1}\mid H_{t+1},E_{t+1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(S_{t+1}\mid S_{t},A_{t},C_{t})\cdot P(C_{t+1}\mid H_{t+1},E_{t+1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=R(S_{t},C_{t},A_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=R(S_{t},C_{t},A_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Approximate sufficiency.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We require the action-level divergence to be small:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi^{\ast}(\cdot\mid S_{t},H_{t},E_{t})\,\big\|\,\pi^{\ast}(\cdot\mid S_{t},C_{t})\right)\leq\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\pi^{\ast}(\cdot\mid S_{t},H_{t},E_{t})\,\big\|\,\pi^{\ast}(\cdot\mid S_{t},C_{t})\right)\leq\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves decision-relevant signal up to tolerance <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice we estimate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> via a lightweight discriminator between the two conditionals and regularize training with a penalty term (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS7" title="3.7 Practical Choices, Robustness, and Interpretability ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Information-Theoretic Objective and Variational Bounds</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We optimize equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2 Problem Reformulation and Design Requirements ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For the MI term we use variational lower bounds with a unified critic <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MINE.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(S;C)=\mathrm{KL}(p(s,c)\|p(s)p(c))\;\geq\;\sup_{f_{\phi}}\;\mathbb{E}_{p(s,c)}[f_{\phi}(s,c)]-\log\mathbb{E}_{p(s)p(c)}\!\left[e^{f_{\phi}(s,c)}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(S;C)=\mathrm{KL}(p(s,c)\|p(s)p(c))\;\geq\;\sup_{f_{\phi}}\;\mathbb{E}_{p(s,c)}[f_{\phi}(s,c)]-\log\mathbb{E}_{p(s)p(c)}\!\left[e^{f_{\phi}(s,c)}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">InfoNCE (in-batch negatives).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(S;C)\;\geq\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\log\frac{\exp(f_{\phi}(s,c))}{\exp(f_{\phi}(s,c))+\sum_{k=1}^{K}\exp\!\big(f_{\phi}(s,c_{k}^{-})\big)}\right]+\log(K{+}1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(S;C)\;\geq\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[\log\frac{\exp(f_{\phi}(s,c))}{\exp(f_{\phi}(s,c))+\sum_{k=1}^{K}\exp\!\big(f_{\phi}(s,c_{k}^{-})\big)}\right]+\log(K{+}1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{k}^{-}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{k}^{-}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are in-batch negatives; unless specified we set <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to the batch size minus one.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Latency–entropy coupling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model decision latency as a power law of context entropy,</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\,H(C_{t})^{\alpha},\qquad\alpha\in[1,\,1.5]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Latency</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\,H(C_{t})^{\alpha},\qquad\alpha\in[1,\,1.5],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and adopt the Lagrangian counterpart of equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2 Problem Reformulation and Design Requirements ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\psi,\theta)=-\,I_{\phi}(S;C)+\lambda\,H(C_{t})+\mu\,[\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})-\mathcal{B}]_{+}+\nu\,[\mathrm{Tokens}(C_{t})-\mathcal{T}]_{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Latency</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Tokens</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\psi,\theta)=-\,I_{\phi}(S;C)+\lambda\,H(C_{t})+\mu\,[\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})-\mathcal{B}]_{+}+\nu\,[\mathrm{Tokens}(C_{t})-\mathcal{T}]_{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimize</em> <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to <em id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maximizing</em> equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.2 Problem Reformulation and Design Requirements ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Context sufficiency and information gain.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To motivate these bounds, let <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the information gain from
context <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The regret between the full-context optimal policy <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and a
partial-context policy <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Regret}(T)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\!\left[V^{\pi^{*}}(\tilde{S}_{t})-V^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Regret}(T)=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\!\left[V^{\pi^{*}}(\tilde{S}_{t})-V^{\pi}(\tilde{S}_{t})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}_{t}=(S_{t},C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}_{t}=(S_{t},C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Maximizing the mutual information
<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(S;C)=H(S)-H(S|C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(S;C)=H(S)-H(S|C)</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the uncertainty about optimal actions and hence lowers regret.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Time–complexity trade-off.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While richer context reduces uncertainty, excessive context increases processing cost.
We denote the computational overhead as a function of context entropy and feature
dimension, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(f(H(C_{t}),d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(f(H(C_{t}),d))</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to a complexity-aware regret bound:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Regret}(T)\;\leq\;O\!\left(\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{k}H(C_{t})\,T}\cdot f(H(C_{t}),d)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi>O</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Regret}(T)\;\leq\;O\!\left(\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{k}H(C_{t})\,T}\cdot f(H(C_{t}),d)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which balances decision quality against latency and token budgets introduced
in equation <a href="#S3.E11" title="In Latency–entropy coupling. ‣ 3.4 Information-Theoretic Objective and Variational Bounds ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumptions and precedent results.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt standard smoothness or Lipschitz‐type assumptions on rewards and transitions, and assume bounded capacity (covering numbers) for the value/policy classes. Such conditions are common in regret analysis for contextual bandits and reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Magureanu et al. (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krishnamurthy et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
Recent information‐theoretic analyses also relate regret to prior entropy or mutual information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neu et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Foster et al. (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Regret scaling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under standard Lipschitz/covering assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Magureanu et al. (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krishnamurthy et al. (2020)</a></cite>,
and following information‐theoretic treatments of contextual bandits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neu et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Foster et al. (2020)</a></cite>,
regret satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Regret}(T)\;\lesssim\;\sqrt{T}\,\Gamma\!\big(H(C_{t}),d\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≲</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.584em;" width="0.584em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Regret}(T)\;\lesssim\;\sqrt{T}\,\Gamma\!\big(H(C_{t}),d\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Refined regret bound)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose equation <a href="#S3.E7" title="In Approximate sufficiency. ‣ 3.3 Summarization-Based State Augmentation ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> holds and the value/policy classes admit an effective dimension <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., the effective rank of the feature covariance). Then for constants <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},c_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},c_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Regret}(T)\;\leq\;c_{1}\,\sqrt{T\,d_{\mathrm{eff}}}\;+\;c_{2}\,\sqrt{T}\,H(C_{t})^{\alpha/2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Regret</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Regret}(T)\;\leq\;c_{1}\,\sqrt{T\,d_{\mathrm{eff}}}\;+\;c_{2}\,\sqrt{T}\,H(C_{t})^{\alpha/2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Complexity-Aware Integration and Update Policy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We deploy a <em id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">budgeted</em> update policy for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:
(i) <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Per-Step</span>; (ii) <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sliding-Window</span> (size <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>); (iii) <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Periodic</span> (interval <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>).
These strategies operationalize the trade-off between richer context and computational overhead identified in Sec. 3.4.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Policy over updates.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Introduce a meta-action <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}\in\{\textsc{Keep},\textsc{Refresh},\textsc{Compress}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Keep</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Refresh</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Compress</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}\in\{\textsc{Keep},\textsc{Refresh},\textsc{Compress}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with meta-policy</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi(A_{t},U_{t}\mid S_{t},H_{t},E_{t})=\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid S_{t},C_{t})\;\cdot\;\pi_{\varphi}(U_{t}\mid\xi_{t}),\qquad C_{t+1}=g_{\psi}(H_{t+1},E_{t+1};U_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.335em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(A_{t},U_{t}\mid S_{t},H_{t},E_{t})=\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid S_{t},C_{t})\;\cdot\;\pi_{\varphi}(U_{t}\mid\xi_{t}),\qquad C_{t+1}=g_{\psi}(H_{t+1},E_{t+1};U_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> aggregates budget/complexity signals (e.g., <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, token count, recent latency, and summary age).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution loop.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At each step:
(1) observe <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S_{t},H_{t},E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S_{t},H_{t},E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>;
(2) compute <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (pre-action);
(3) act <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid S_{t},C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid S_{t},C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>;
(4) observe <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R_{t},S_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R_{t},S_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> and update <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>;
(5) decide <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and refresh <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stability of contextual integration.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure decisions are robust to redundant or noisy features, we measure stability as</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Stability}(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})=\max_{i}\left\|\,h(\tilde{S}_{t}\circ\alpha_{+i})-h(\tilde{S}_{t})\,\right\|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Stability</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Stability}(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})=\max_{i}\left\|\,h(\tilde{S}_{t}\circ\alpha_{+i})-h(\tilde{S}_{t})\,\right\|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the decision outcome and <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{+i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{+i}</annotation></semantics></math> injects an additional context feature.
Low values indicate that small perturbations in context do not significantly alter actions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Complexity-Aware Summarization for Context-Augmented MDPs</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> policy <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, summarizer <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, budgets <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, update mode <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{\textsc{Per-Step},\textsc{Window}(W),\textsc{Periodic}(k)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Per-Step</mtext><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Window</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Periodic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{\textsc{Per-Step},\textsc{Window}(W),\textsc{Periodic}(k)\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,2,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,2,\dots</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
                
                Observe <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t},H_{t},E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t},H_{t},E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>;  compute <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}\!\leftarrow\!g_{\psi}(H_{t},E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}\!\leftarrow\!g_{\psi}(H_{t},E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> s.t. <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tokens}(C_{t})\!\leq\!\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Tokens</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tokens}(C_{t})\!\leq\!\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})\!\leq\!\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Latency</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Latency}(C_{t})\!\leq\!\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
                
                Sample <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{t}\!\sim\!\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid S_{t},C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}\!\sim\!\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid S_{t},C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, execute, observe <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R_{t},S_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R_{t},S_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math>; update <math id="alg1.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
                
                Form <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, token count, latency, age); sample <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="U_{t}\!\sim\!\pi_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\xi_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}\!\sim\!\pi_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\xi_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>; refresh <math id="alg1.l5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t+1}\!\leftarrow\!g_{\psi}(H_{t+1},E_{t+1};U_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t+1}\!\leftarrow\!g_{\psi}(H_{t+1},E_{t+1};U_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
                Update <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> using MI bound equation <a href="#S3.E8" title="In MINE. ‣ 3.4 Information-Theoretic Objective and Variational Bounds ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>/equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="In InfoNCE (in-batch negatives). ‣ 3.4 Information-Theoretic Objective and Variational Bounds ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and the Lagrangian penalty equation <a href="#S3.E11" title="In Latency–entropy coupling. ‣ 3.4 Information-Theoretic Objective and Variational Bounds ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                
                
                Update <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> by policy gradient: <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}J=\mathbb{E}[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid\tilde{S}_{t})\,\hat{A}_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}J=\mathbb{E}[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid\tilde{S}_{t})\,\hat{A}_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Policy Gradient and Value Learning</h3>

<section id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Policy gradient.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid\tilde{S}_{t})\,\hat{A}_{t}\right],\qquad\hat{A}_{t}=Q^{\pi_{\theta}}(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})-b(\tilde{S}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid\tilde{S}_{t})\,\hat{A}_{t}\right],\qquad\hat{A}_{t}=Q^{\pi_{\theta}}(\tilde{S}_{t},A_{t})-b(\tilde{S}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">DQN objective (augmented state).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\ast}(\tilde{s},a)=R(\tilde{s},a)+\gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{s}^{\prime}}\!\left[\max_{a^{\prime}}Q^{\ast}(\tilde{s}^{\prime},a^{\prime})\right],\qquad\mathcal{L}_{Q}=\mathbb{E}\Big[(Q_{\omega}-(R_{t}+\gamma\max_{a^{\prime}}Q_{\bar{\omega}}))^{2}\Big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\ast}(\tilde{s},a)=R(\tilde{s},a)+\gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{s}^{\prime}}\!\left[\max_{a^{\prime}}Q^{\ast}(\tilde{s}^{\prime},a^{\prime})\right],\qquad\mathcal{L}_{Q}=\mathbb{E}\Big[(Q_{\omega}-(R_{t}+\gamma\max_{a^{\prime}}Q_{\bar{\omega}}))^{2}\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the target-network parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.7 </span>Practical Choices, Robustness, and Interpretability</h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token budgeting &amp; padding.</span> Unless noted, summaries are capped at <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{32,64,128\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>128</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{32,64,128\}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. Embeddings are truncated or zero-padded to match the configured <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\,\mathrm{dim}(C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>dim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\,\mathrm{dim}(C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Update frequency.</span> <span id="S3.SS7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sliding-Window</span> (<math id="S3.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W{=}16</annotation></semantics></math>) mitigates bursty costs; <span id="S3.SS7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Periodic</span> (<math id="S3.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}8</annotation></semantics></math>) favors minimal latency; <span id="S3.SS7.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Per-Step</span> maximizes absolute quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitivity regularization.</span></p>
<table id="S3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{C_{t}}Q^{\pi}(S_{t},C_{t},A_{t})\|_{2}\leq L_{Q},\qquad\|\nabla_{C_{t}}\log\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid S_{t},C_{t})\|_{2}\leq L_{\pi}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{C_{t}}Q^{\pi}(S_{t},C_{t},A_{t})\|_{2}\leq L_{Q},\qquad\|\nabla_{C_{t}}\log\pi_{\theta}(A_{t}\mid S_{t},C_{t})\|_{2}\leq L_{\pi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sufficiency regularizer.</span> We add <math id="S3.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{suff}}\cdot\hat{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>suff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{suff}}\cdot\hat{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from a discriminator estimating equation <a href="#S3.E7" title="In Approximate sufficiency. ‣ 3.3 Summarization-Based State Augmentation ‣ 3 Method ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interpretability.</span> We log top-<math id="S3.SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> salient phrases contributing to <math id="S3.SS7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (via attribution on the embedder) and align them with action shifts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.8 </span>Joint Training Objective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\underbrace{\mathcal{L}_{\text{RL}}(\theta,\omega)}_{\text{PG or TD}}+\eta_{1}\Big(-I_{\phi}(S;C)\Big)+\eta_{2}\,H(C_{t})+\eta_{3}\,\mathcal{R}_{\text{sens}}+\eta_{4}\,\mathcal{R}_{\text{budget}}+\eta_{5}\,\hat{\varepsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>PG or TD</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>sens</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>budget</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ε</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\underbrace{\mathcal{L}_{\text{RL}}(\theta,\omega)}_{\text{PG or TD}}+\eta_{1}\Big(-I_{\phi}(S;C)\Big)+\eta_{2}\,H(C_{t})+\eta_{3}\,\mathcal{R}_{\text{sens}}+\eta_{4}\,\mathcal{R}_{\text{budget}}+\eta_{5}\,\hat{\varepsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Default weights.</em> Unless otherwise stated we use <math id="S3.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{2}{\in}[10^{-3},10^{-2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{2}{\in}[10^{-3},10^{-2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{3}{=}10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{3}{=}10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{4}{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{4}{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{5}{=}10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{5}{=}10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> with linear warm-up over the first 10% of training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Datasets and Evaluation Metrics</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Dataset</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Dataset ‣ 4 Datasets and Evaluation Metrics ‣ Learning to Decide with Just Enough: Information-Theoretic Context Summarization for CMDPs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the datasets and environments used for evaluation. The suite covers a diverse range of modalities and decision making settings: structured tabular data, textual context, discrete planning, visual control, continuous control, and recommendation. For Drug Discovery Optimization, we curate a high-dimensional molecular dataset with associated textual annotations to simulate realistic exogenous signals. FrozenLake and Contextual Multi-Armed Bandit provide controlled stochastic environments for testing sample efficiency and exploration. Atari Pong and Breakout represent high-dimensional visual inputs with temporal dependencies. MuJoCo tasks (HalfCheetah and Ant) evaluate continuous locomotion with long-horizon credit assignment. MovieLens provides implicit-feedback recommendation data, testing our method under sparse rewards and large action spaces. This diversity ensures that improvements cannot be attributed to a single modality or environment type.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:56.3pt;vertical-align:-26.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-101.7pt,14.4pt) scale(0.661544930386945,0.661544930386945) ;">
